# Loaded by `npm run dev`. Committed on purpose: contains no secrets, only local wiring.

VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080/api/v1
VITE_API_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
VITE_APP_VERSION=0.1.0-dev

# Mocks ON by default.
#
# The Spring Boot service currently implements /health and nothing else, and denies every
# other path by design. With mocks off, signing in calls a real endpoint that does not
# exist and comes back 401 "Your session is not valid" — which reads like a broken login
# rather than a missing backend.
#
# Run `npm run dev:live` to point at the real API instead. Flip this to false once the
# backend implements auth.
VITE_ENABLE_MOCK_API=true

# Which parts of the API go to the REAL Spring Boot service. Everything else stays mocked.
#
# Why this exists: with mocks on, a request never leaves the browser — MSW answers it — so
# no verification email is sent and no real session is created. With mocks off entirely
# (`npm run dev:live`) the implemented flows are real, but the dashboard, accounts,
# transfers and the staff portal all return 401, because the backend does not implement
# them yet. This is the middle setting.
#
# Comma-separated path prefixes, relative to /api/v1. Implemented on the backend today:
#
#   registration/personal   register, verify the email, submit the application
#   registration/otp        resend the verification code
#
# NOT `registration` on its own, and this is a trap worth understanding.
#
# The backend implements /registration/personal/* and /registration/otp/resend. It does
# NOT implement /registration/business or /registration/join — company onboarding is
# still a mock. Listing the whole `registration` prefix sent those two to the real
# service as well, where Spring Security's catch-all denies any path it does not
# recognise. Company registration therefore failed with "Your account does not have
# permission to do this" — a permission error for a missing feature, which is the most
# misleading message the system can produce.
#
# So the prefixes name exactly what exists. Multi-segment prefixes work, and the segment
# check means `registration/personal` does not accidentally match anything else.
#   auth           sign in, the one-time code, the forced password change, sign out
#   session        who is signed in, and whether they must change their password
#                  (switching to a corporate profile is not implemented, and personal
#                  accounts never use it)
#   admin          the staff portal: applications, customers, sent messages, the
#                  overview counts, and the development reset button
#   accounts       the customer's own accounts: the list, one account, and its
#                  transactions (always empty - this service holds no ledger)
#
# NOTE ON `accounts`. Leaving it off is what made a customer see nothing while staff saw
# both their accounts: MSW answered /accounts from a fixture that matches no real
# customer, so the request never reached the service that had the data. Everything under
# the prefix must exist on the backend before listing it - /accounts, /accounts/{id} and
# /accounts/{id}/transactions all do.
#
# A prefix that matches no mocked endpoint stops the app at start-up rather than quietly
# leaving that area mocked, so a typo here is loud.
#
# To use it:
#   1. List the areas you want live below.
#   2. Start the API, with a database that KEEPS what you register:
#          cd ../backend && docker compose up -d db
#          ./mvnw spring-boot:run -Dspring-boot.run.profiles=dev
#      (with MAIL_ENABLED=true and MAIL_PASSWORD set in backend/.env)
#
#      The `h2` profile also works and needs no Docker, but its database is IN MEMORY:
#      every customer you register is gone the next time the API restarts. Fine for a
#      quick look, wrong for walking the onboarding flow over a few days.
#      No Docker? See backend/docs/LOCAL-DATABASE.md.
#   3. Register with any account number of 10 to 16 digits. The backend no longer checks
#      the number against a bank record: matching an applicant to an existing Zigama
#      client is a manual step the manager performs before approving, using systems this
#      service is not connected to. The format is all that is validated here, and the
#      10-16 range is a GUESS the bank still has to confirm (docs/OPEN-ITEMS.md).
#   4. Use a real email address you can read. The code arrives there.
#
# The full onboarding walk needs registration, auth and session together, because the
# temporary password is issued by one and spent by the others:
#   VITE_LIVE_API=registration/personal,registration/otp,auth,session
VITE_LIVE_API=registration/personal,registration/otp,auth,session,admin,accounts,transactions/recent,beneficiaries,service-requests,security,notifications
